WEBVTT

00:00:00.761 --> 00:00:03.423
The example in this part
needs no graphics card,

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:07.046
no voice server and, once
the tools are built,

00:00:07.626 --> 00:00:08.867
no internet connection.

00:00:09.848 --> 00:00:12.390
Its narration is silent, on purpose:

00:00:12.971 --> 00:00:14.572
a placeholder with the right length,

00:00:15.153 --> 00:00:19.416
so the pictures and the video come out
exactly as they will with a real voice.

00:00:20.797 --> 00:00:24.320
You can see that work before you
record or clone your own voice.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.881
This part of the series
answers two questions:

00:00:28.481 --> 00:00:31.202
what do you need, and can
you see it work first?

00:00:32.122 --> 00:00:33.562
I will go through the short list,

00:00:34.082 --> 00:00:35.943
get the project and a coding agent,

00:00:36.543 --> 00:00:38.383
set up your list of names to block,

00:00:38.984 --> 00:00:41.144
build the tools, run the tests,

00:00:41.464 --> 00:00:42.564
and render the example.

00:00:43.825 --> 00:00:49.346
The voice, the slowest step and the one a
graphics card speeds up most, comes later.

00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:53.708
The short list starts
with the machine itself,

00:00:54.309 --> 00:00:56.609
because everything else runs on top of it.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:02.692
The only system I tested is Linux on
an ordinary Intel or AMD processor.

00:01:04.052 --> 00:01:09.454
Apple machines, Windows machines and machines
with other kinds of chip are untested,

00:01:10.035 --> 00:01:11.255
so I will not promise them.

00:01:12.776 --> 00:01:16.537
You need Docker with Compose version
two point two four or newer,

00:01:17.117 --> 00:01:20.298
because every pipeline stage
runs inside a container,

00:01:20.879 --> 00:01:24.780
and you do not install Python
or the audio tools yourself.

00:01:26.301 --> 00:01:29.822
A graphics card is optional,
and it has to be an NVIDIA one:

00:01:30.442 --> 00:01:35.304
the voice is faster on it, but every
stage also has a processor-only version.

00:01:36.824 --> 00:01:39.206
With the machine ready, get the project.

00:01:40.347 --> 00:01:44.049
Clone the repository with git,
and step into its folder.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:49.212
Next, install Claude Code, the
coding agent this series uses.

00:01:50.913 --> 00:01:54.756
On Linux the installer is a single
line that you paste into a terminal,

00:01:55.356 --> 00:01:58.118
and asking for its version
tells you it worked.

00:01:59.158 --> 00:02:01.560
Then start it from inside
the project folder,

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.322
and it asks you to sign in the first time.

00:02:05.622 --> 00:02:11.286
It needs a paid Claude plan, a Console
account, or access through a cloud provider.

00:02:13.146 --> 00:02:15.687
Now you can ask the agent
to do the setup for you.

00:02:17.468 --> 00:02:19.168
This is the prompt I would start with:

00:02:19.748 --> 00:02:21.789
read the readme and the full-run page,

00:02:22.269 --> 00:02:24.290
check my machine against the requirements,

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.130
and tell me what is missing.

00:02:27.451 --> 00:02:29.571
Do not install anything without asking.

00:02:31.352 --> 00:02:35.013
Then copy the settings files,
ask me for the names to block,

00:02:35.593 --> 00:02:37.794
build the image, run the tests,

00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:39.895
and render the offline example,

00:02:40.455 --> 00:02:42.816
and at any failure stop
and show me the error.

00:02:44.316 --> 00:02:46.277
I ran that prompt in a fresh copy,

00:02:46.817 --> 00:02:51.178
in a read-only mode where the agent
may read files but not run commands.

00:02:52.659 --> 00:02:55.079
It gave the same steps I ran by hand,

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:57.920
and it came back with the
right questions first:

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.381
is Docker installed, which names to block,

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:04.162
and do I want the draft
or the final render.

00:03:05.563 --> 00:03:06.883
I did not let it run them.

00:03:08.243 --> 00:03:11.604
The next scenes are what it
does, so you can check its work.

00:03:13.664 --> 00:03:17.766
The first thing the agent will ask you
for is the one thing that protects you.

00:03:19.406 --> 00:03:23.147
This pipeline reads your scripts
and puts words on screen,

00:03:23.747 --> 00:03:26.548
so it needs to know which
names must never appear.

00:03:27.949 --> 00:03:30.229
Copy the example list of names to block,

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:32.910
and put your own in it: machine names,

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:35.071
your user name, family names.

00:03:36.791 --> 00:03:41.693
The safety gate is a check that runs before
the main stages and stops them if one of

00:03:41.733 --> 00:03:43.754
those names shows up in a script,

00:03:44.274 --> 00:03:45.754
a scene or a subtitle.

00:03:46.875 --> 00:03:51.596
Until you fill the list in, every step
that checks prints a loud warning:

00:03:52.216 --> 00:03:55.597
the gate still runs, but it
does not know your names.

00:03:57.038 --> 00:04:01.659
Then switch on the git hooks, small
checks that git runs before every commit.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:07.381
They refuse a commit that includes
your private files or any audio,

00:04:08.001 --> 00:04:10.382
but they do not read the
text of your scripts.

00:04:11.722 --> 00:04:13.643
The gate does that when you run a stage.

00:04:14.983 --> 00:04:19.305
With the names protected, you can build
the container that holds every tool.

00:04:20.365 --> 00:04:22.726
Before the build, write two small files:

00:04:23.306 --> 00:04:24.926
a settings file for the tools,

00:04:25.507 --> 00:04:30.048
which you copy from the example and which
keeps the voice in its silent test mode,

00:04:30.628 --> 00:04:33.949
and your user id, so the
container runs as you.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.130
Then start the build
and do something else.

00:04:38.171 --> 00:04:41.972
From a clean copy it took about three
and a half minutes on my connection,

00:04:42.552 --> 00:04:44.733
and the image is about eight gigabytes.

00:04:46.314 --> 00:04:52.240
When the build finishes, run the test suite inside
the container before you trust anything else.

00:04:53.921 --> 00:04:54.821
It takes seconds.

00:04:56.043 --> 00:04:59.607
Look for zero failures, and
then the word OK near the end.

00:05:00.808 --> 00:05:03.210
If it does not pass, fix that first:

00:05:03.791 --> 00:05:06.314
nothing after it is worth
debugging on a broken base.

00:05:07.814 --> 00:05:10.295
Now the offline run, one stage at a time.

00:05:11.556 --> 00:05:13.097
First the silent narration.

00:05:14.037 --> 00:05:16.678
Then alignment, which finds
when each word is said.

00:05:18.219 --> 00:05:21.920
Then the scene briefs, a short
description of each picture,

00:05:22.401 --> 00:05:25.382
and keyframes, still frames
to check the layout.

00:05:26.843 --> 00:05:29.944
Last, a draft render of
the moving pictures,

00:05:30.544 --> 00:05:32.865
and a draft video that
joins them with the sound.

00:05:34.586 --> 00:05:37.967
Each stage prints a last line
you can compare with the docs.

00:05:39.108 --> 00:05:41.109
The draft render took about a minute once,

00:05:41.689 --> 00:05:45.111
on a machine with twenty-four
cores and no graphics card,

00:05:45.691 --> 00:05:47.151
and about two on a copy.

00:05:48.472 --> 00:05:50.453
This runs on the example where it ships.

00:05:51.914 --> 00:05:54.535
Your own episodes go
in an episodes folder,

00:05:54.995 --> 00:05:57.256
which a fresh copy does not have yet,

00:05:57.836 --> 00:05:59.977
and the docs show the one
command that makes it.

00:06:01.758 --> 00:06:03.639
Three things are missing from this render,

00:06:04.239 --> 00:06:07.280
and each one costs you something
the offline run did not:

00:06:07.881 --> 00:06:10.082
a network, disk space, or money.

00:06:10.982 --> 00:06:14.504
First, the network:
alignment with a real voice,

00:06:15.085 --> 00:06:16.525
and the checks that listen to it,

00:06:17.126 --> 00:06:19.467
download their models the
first time you use them.

00:06:20.487 --> 00:06:25.330
Second, disk space: the voice server
downloads its own weights on first start,

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:28.412
about three gigabytes
for one voice option,

00:06:28.992 --> 00:06:30.193
on top of its own image.

00:06:31.113 --> 00:06:36.036
Third, money: the scenes, the script critique
and the reviews are written by a model

00:06:36.076 --> 00:06:38.277
in the cloud, through the coding agent,

00:06:38.857 --> 00:06:41.519
and I have not worked out
what an episode costs,

00:06:42.079 --> 00:06:43.039
so I will not guess.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.561
If a model in the cloud
already writes the scenes,

00:06:48.141 --> 00:06:52.183
it is fair to ask why the rest
does not run in the cloud as well.

00:06:53.523 --> 00:06:55.044
The voice I use is the reason:

00:06:55.644 --> 00:07:00.485
it needs a graphics card, and a proper
graphics card in the cloud for the coding

00:07:00.565 --> 00:07:03.867
agent to use is far more
involved than you would think.

00:07:04.747 --> 00:07:06.447
Your recording of your own voice,

00:07:07.068 --> 00:07:11.549
your list of names to block and your
sign-in for uploading to YouTube are yours,

00:07:12.109 --> 00:07:14.150
and I did not want them in more places.

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:20.212
That is my judgement, not a test: I
have not tried a sandbox in the cloud.

00:07:21.572 --> 00:07:23.053
I will say when the cloud comes in.

00:07:24.573 --> 00:07:28.915
When you are ready for your own series,
give the agent a second prompt.

00:07:30.276 --> 00:07:34.637
Copy the template rules file into the
project as the agent's own rules,

00:07:35.218 --> 00:07:36.178
with your series name.

00:07:37.478 --> 00:07:41.080
Create the first episode folder
with the project's new command,

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:45.902
and ask me what problem the episode
solves before writing anything.

00:07:47.342 --> 00:07:49.103
I ran that one read-only as well.

00:07:49.904 --> 00:07:51.584
The agent gave me the right order:

00:07:52.104 --> 00:07:54.225
the rules file, your list of names,

00:07:54.285 --> 00:07:57.787
the new episode folder, then
a plan, then the facts.

00:07:59.207 --> 00:08:02.749
It held until I approved,
before any script was written,

00:08:03.329 --> 00:08:04.970
and that is the habit to keep,

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:08.611
because you approve the script
before any voice is recorded.

00:08:10.152 --> 00:08:13.034
A silent render proves the
pictures and the video.

00:08:14.395 --> 00:08:18.137
It does not prove the voice,
because there is no voice to check,

00:08:18.718 --> 00:08:20.959
so the checks that listen refuse to run.

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:24.101
Next is the plan for an episode,

00:08:24.502 --> 00:08:26.203
which the project calls the spec:

00:08:26.783 --> 00:08:29.045
how one problem becomes one episode,

00:08:29.625 --> 00:08:31.086
and what never goes on screen.
